## 1. Scope

### 1.1 In Scope
The scope of this strategy covers the authenticated avatar upload and crop capability for public profile display, including all functional and non-functional controls that govern how a single high-resolution avatar image is accepted, validated, processed, stored, propagated, and rendered. The feature is not treated as a simple file upload widget; it is a multi-layer transaction that begins with browser session evaluation, proceeds through API Gateway enforcement, performs synchronous malware scanning and deep file signature validation, persists only approved content into the authorized cloud bucket hierarchy, and completes with immediate CDN cache invalidation and dynamic user interface synchronization across active user views. Because the business objective is recognizability of account identity across the member experience, test scope must confirm not only successful upload, but also the visible and timely distribution of the updated image to profile views and header surfaces without a full browser reload.

The in-scope behavior includes authentication gating of the avatar modification user interface, ensuring that only users with a fresh, unexpired browser cookie session token can view and interact with the upload and crop controls. It includes request rejection behavior at the gateway for unauthenticated and expired-token traffic, exact file-size boundary validation where 5.00MB payloads are accepted and 5.01MB payloads are rejected, strict JPEG and WebP support enforcement, magic-number inspection to reject spoofed file extensions, synchronous inline malware scanning prior to persistence, storage into specialized cloud bucket folders, immediate CDN invalidation after write success, and near-real-time propagation to profile and header-bar rendering surfaces. Cropping capability is explicitly in scope and will be validated as a server-side operation, including crop parameter handling, persistence of the cropped result, and preview-to-final consistency.

The strategy further includes UI state validation, API contract verification, service and integration testing, end-to-end system validation, performance testing under standard 4G throttling, security verification around ingress controls, fault-injection exercises for downstream dependency instability, audit-log verification, and release-readiness evidence generation. Because acceptance criteria require completion of the upload-to-distribution path within 2.0 seconds, scope also includes stage-level latency measurement and attribution across transport, scan, storage, cache purge, and propagation phases. The strategy therefore addresses both user-visible outcomes and hidden operational controls that could create release risk if left unverified.

### 1.2 Out of Scope
Areas outside this strategy remain structurally excluded to preserve requirement fidelity and to avoid false assumptions regarding product capability. Client-side compression, local scaling, browser canvas-based transformation engines, and any image manipulation performed on the user device are out of scope because the approved architecture explicitly mandates server-side-only processing. The front end is expected to transmit raw image binaries and render state changes, but it is not expected to alter the binary payload, optimize dimensions locally, or execute browser-based image processing. Therefore, test design will actively confirm absence of client-side processing dependencies rather than introducing coverage for unsupported browser transformation functions.

Alternative image formats including PNG, SVG, GIF, and raw design files are also out of scope for positive compatibility coverage. They remain in negative validation coverage only, to confirm rejection behavior and whitelist enforcement. Likewise, concurrent batch uploads, multi-image profile header modifications, and any form of multi-select upload semantics are outside the defined business boundary because the current feature supports single-avatar submission only. Storage infrastructure outside the authorized cloud ecosystem is not a supported target and is excluded from solution compatibility coverage, though the strategy will validate that writes do not occur to unauthorized destinations.

External networks, unsupported deployment targets, and speculative future workflow variants are excluded from this release strategy because they would dilute traceability to the approved acceptance criteria. Any enterprise concerns not directly mapped to the current avatar ingestion flow, such as media moderation queues beyond inline malware scanning, generalized DAM workflows, or admin-driven asset backfills, are not covered here.

## 2. Test Approach

### 2.1 Testing Levels
Testing will be executed across Component/UI, API, Service/Integration, System End-to-End, Security Gateway/Ingress, Non-Functional Performance, and Security & Audit Log Verification levels. This layered model is necessary because the feature’s risk profile is distributed across user experience, request routing, content validation, persistence controls, cache coherence, and latency budget compliance. Component and UI testing will prove state behavior for hidden and disabled controls when the session is absent or expired, validate crop interactions, and confirm dynamic DOM mutation after a successful update. API and gateway testing will verify contract compliance, status codes, early rejection rules, and prevention of unauthorized requests from reaching downstream services.

Integration and system testing will validate the multi-hop path from upload initiation through gateway, malware scanning, deep header validation, storage commit, CDN purge, and visual propagation. Security verification will focus on spoofed-file interception, malware-enforcement ordering, token validity handling, and audit trail completeness. Performance testing will be conducted under standard 4G throttling to validate deterministic SLA adherence and to identify whether transport, scanner latency, object storage write time, or CDN propagation causes budget erosion.

### 2.2 Test Design Techniques
The strategy applies boundary value analysis for the strict 5MB threshold, equivalence partitioning for authentication and file-type classes, decision-table testing for combinations of session status, MIME validity, file header integrity, malware result, storage outcome, and CDN invalidation result, and state-transition testing for visibility and enabled-state behavior of avatar controls. Negative testing is central to this release because many failure conditions must be blocked before persistence, including unauthenticated access, expired tokens, spoofed files, malformed headers, unsupported types, malware-positive submissions, and duplicate or parallel submission attempts.

Path and flow testing will be used to prove correct execution order, specifically that gateway authorization occurs before scanner invocation, that scanning and header validation occur before storage, and that cache invalidation only occurs after successful persistence. DOM mutation observation and browser navigation monitoring will confirm that avatar updates are rendered without full page reloads. Fault injection will be introduced for scanner delay, storage write failure, and CDN invalidation lag in order to validate resilience controls and release readiness under realistic instability conditions.

## 3. Quality Objectives

### 3.1 Functional Quality Objectives
The primary functional quality objective is to ensure that the avatar upload and crop capability behaves as a tightly controlled authenticated workflow rather than a loosely validated media submission. The product must reliably expose the upload and crop interface only to users possessing a fresh, unexpired session token, and it must reject unauthorized or expired requests at the gateway with HTTP 401 before any downstream processing is invoked. This objective is critical because a defect in access gating would represent both a security weakness and an architectural breach, allowing unnecessary load on malware scanning or storage services and potentially exposing profile asset mutation to unauthorized traffic. Therefore, functional quality is defined not only by user-facing correctness but also by correct transaction ordering and suppression of forbidden side effects.

A second functional objective is to ensure deterministic content validation and transformation handling. The system must accept only JPEG and WebP files, validate file authenticity via structural header inspection rather than extension naming, reject spoofed or malicious binaries, and preserve correct crop semantics when the user defines a valid crop area. Crop behavior must be treated as part of the final stored result, not merely a UI preview. Functional quality therefore includes validating that crop coordinates are honored server-side, invalid crop values are rejected safely, previewed selections correspond to the final persisted avatar, and propagated views display the intended final crop.

The third functional objective is synchronized experience consistency. Once a valid avatar is stored, the image must propagate across active profile views and header bars without full page reload, and the visible state must align with back-end persistence and CDN purge events. A successful upload that stores correctly but leaves stale header imagery or delayed propagation will be classified as a functional failure because it violates recognizability goals and undermines user trust in profile updates.

### 3.2 Non-Functional Quality Objectives
The dominant non-functional quality objective is meeting the 2.0 second end-to-end SLA under standard 4G network conditions. This metric covers not just upload completion, but the full user-observable path: upload transport, gateway handling, synchronous malware scan, deep header validation, storage commit, cache invalidation, and public view refresh. To make this auditable, the strategy will collect stage timestamps and evaluate repeatability across multiple runs, including warm and cold conditions where applicable. Success requires the overall path to remain within the allowed threshold while preserving security and validation rigor; no optimization that weakens malware scanning or header verification is acceptable.

Security and operational integrity form the second non-functional objective. The system must prevent spoofed files from bypassing validation, ensure malware scanning occurs inline and synchronously before persistence, preserve auditability of all rejection and success events, and prevent unauthorized storage destinations or bypass routing. The release will be considered unfit if logs are incomplete, if dependency failures produce ambiguous outcomes, or if unauthorized requests can invoke downstream services despite correct status code responses. Non-functional quality therefore includes observability, tamper-evident audit records, and deterministic failure isolation.

A third non-functional objective is resilience under realistic dependency behavior. The flow depends on multiple services that may experience latency spikes, stale token windows, cache purge lag, or transient storage errors. The strategy will therefore validate graceful handling, controlled failure surfaces, accurate user messaging, and absence of partial-success corruption states. Quality in this context means that the system fails safely, leaves no orphan side effects, and provides operationally actionable evidence for triage.

## 4. Test Strategy

### 4.1 Test Coverage
This strategy maps each approved business objective into executable coverage at the most suitable layer, while preserving full traceability to acceptance criteria and architectural constraints. Coverage includes authentication gating, request rejection ordering, precise size threshold handling, MIME and magic-number validation, malware inspection sequencing, server-side-only processing, crop execution and persistence, storage destination enforcement, CDN invalidation, UI synchronization, no-reload rendering, and SLA validation under network throttling. Negative and failure-path coverage is intentionally broad because release risk is concentrated in guardrail enforcement rather than happy-path upload alone.

#### 4.1.1 Test Types
*Describe all the test types in this project and provide the general testing timeline.*

Functional testing will validate upload eligibility, crop submission, gateway responses, storage outcomes, and synchronized avatar refresh across UI surfaces. Negative testing will confirm rejection of expired or absent sessions, unsupported file types, malformed headers, malware-positive payloads, oversize submissions, concurrent multi-upload attempts, and invalid crop parameters. Integration and end-to-end testing will prove the ordered execution across gateway, scanner, storage, CDN, and SPA rendering. Performance testing will validate SLA conformance using repeatable timed runs under 4G constraints. Security testing will verify early access rejection, deep file authenticity checks, malware enforcement, audit logging, and forbidden destination prevention. Regression testing will ensure adjacent profile rendering behavior is not broken by avatar feature changes.

The general timeline will begin with component and API validation in Dev, followed by integration, negative, and security verification in QA, controlled business-flow confirmation in UAT, production-like performance and release-readiness validation in Pre-Prod, and focused smoke verification in Prod after deployment. Automation testing will run continuously in CI for stable scenarios and nightly for heavier integration and performance suites.

