### 4.2 Requirement Traceability Matrix
<Describe how requirements / user stories will be mapped and traced dynamically with test cases.>

Requirement traceability will be managed from the originating user story AD-488 through acceptance criteria, derived test conditions, automated scripts, execution evidence, and defect records. Each structured requirement from the approved scope boundaries is assigned a unique trace key and linked to one or more test cases in the test management repository. For example, authentication gating, 401 gateway rejection, 5.00MB acceptance, 5.01MB rejection, JPEG/WebP-only support, magic-number validation, malware enforcement, crop persistence, authorized bucket storage, CDN invalidation, multi-view synchronization, and no-reload DOM refresh each receive explicit linkage to both positive and negative cases. This dynamic traceability is maintained in Jira using issue links, labels, and test identifiers, with pull requests required to reference the associated story and impacted automated checks.

Operationally, requirement-to-test linkage will be established during test design review and then enforced through CI/CD workflow controls. New or modified automation must include a trace tag in the test metadata naming the corresponding acceptance criterion or derived requirement ID. The pipeline will publish results back to the reporting layer so that failed tests can be seen in direct relation to impacted business outcomes. Where one requirement demands evidence from multiple layers, such as proving unauthorized requests are blocked before downstream invocation, the trace record will aggregate API logs, scanner invocation logs, storage write absence confirmation, and UI error response validation. This prevents shallow one-to-one mapping and supports full release accountability.

Peer review is mandatory for all traceability updates. QA analysts define the initial trace matrix, automation engineers add script references, and release management validates that every release-critical control has evidence at the correct depth. Any requirement without executable coverage or approved rationale enters the release dashboard as a readiness gap. The traceability model therefore functions as both planning mechanism and release gate artifact.

### 4.3 Test Management
<Define the operational test management procedures and execution lifecycle patterns.>

Test management for AD-488 will follow a structured lifecycle spanning test intake, design, peer review, automation implementation, environment qualification, scheduled execution, defect triage, retest, and release evidence sign-off. Work will be orchestrated primarily in Jira, where the parent story AD-488 is linked to task-level design items, test execution cycles, and defects. Test cases will be versioned, with clear ownership assigned to QA analysts for scenario definition and automation engineers for script implementation. Manual exploratory sessions will be logged for high-risk areas such as crop parameter anomalies, edge cache propagation delays, and scanner/service desynchronization. Each execution cycle will include entry criteria, environment status checks, test data availability verification, and observability confirmation before launch.

CI/CD integration is central to execution discipline. Pull requests touching avatar upload, authentication middleware, gateway policies, file validation services, storage routing, or CDN invalidation logic must trigger a mandatory quality workflow. At minimum, this workflow includes unit and configuration checks, targeted PyTest API suites, Playwright UI smoke scenarios, and static security checks. Merge to the integration branch requires successful completion of these checks, peer code review approval, and explicit linkage to the Jira issue. Nightly pipelines extend this with broader regression coverage, including negative file samples, malware simulation cases, crop persistence tests, and DOM synchronization verification. Pre-Prod pipelines add JMeter SLA validation under tcconfig-imposed network constraints and OWASP ZAP security scanning before release readiness can be declared.

Operational governance requires regular triage ceremonies. Daily QA standups review failed tests, environment instability, flaky automation signals, and blocked dependencies. A twice-weekly defect review with engineering and product will classify severity, assign remediation owners, and verify whether defects represent functional, security, performance, or release-readiness blockers. Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 will pause the pipeline after document validation and before production publication or release progression. During this gate, leadership will validate scope completeness, residual risk posture, and waiver requirements. The execution state is therefore intentionally controlled, auditable, and resistant to silent quality erosion.

#### 4.3.2 Test Repositories
<Indicate where the test materials are stored, who manages the repository tool, and how tracking updates are maintained.>

All automated assets for this feature will be stored in the source-controlled quality engineering repository under clearly separated modules for UI, API, performance, and security suites. Playwright scripts for browser interaction and DOM mutation verification will be stored alongside reusable authentication fixtures, upload helpers, and trace tags that map back to AD-488 requirements. PyTest suites will maintain deterministic multipart request builders, file-fixture utilities, gateway contract assertions, and log-correlation helpers that validate whether downstream services were or were not invoked. JMeter test plans will be versioned with parameterized datasets and threshold assertions, while OWASP ZAP baseline and authenticated scan configurations will be maintained as pipeline-executable artifacts. tcconfig profiles for 4G simulation will be stored as reproducible environment definitions.

Repository ownership resides jointly with the QA automation lead and the release engineering function. Branch protections require pull-request submission for all changes, at least one peer reviewer from QA or SDET, and one reviewer from engineering when shared fixtures or environment settings are modified. Direct commits to protected branches are prohibited. Every repository change must reference Jira issue keys, include a concise test-impact note, and preserve backward traceability to acceptance criteria. Test repositories are not passive storage; they are treated as governed release assets with review, audit, and rollback capability.

Tracking updates are maintained through commit history, release tagging, test metadata version stamps, and pipeline-generated execution summaries. When scripts are retired or superseded, deprecation rationale must be documented in the repository README or test catalog. This ensures that over time, the strategy remains maintainable and transparent, particularly for critical controls such as malware ordering, gateway rejection, and SLA enforcement.

#### 4.3.3 Recording Pass/Fail Results
<Indicate the exact criteria for marking an automated or manual test case as pass or fail.>

A test case will be marked Pass only when all expected assertions are satisfied and the evidence demonstrates the correct business and technical outcome at the intended layer. For UI scenarios, this includes visible state, hidden or disabled behavior where required, accurate error banners, correct crop preview/result consistency, DOM mutation verification, and proof that no full page navigation occurred. For API scenarios, a pass requires correct status codes, payload structure, and side-effect confirmation, such as proving unauthorized requests did not invoke malware scanning, storage writes, or CDN purges. For performance scenarios, pass criteria include meeting the agreed threshold distribution, specifically deterministic end-to-end completion within the 2.0 second target under tcconfig-defined 4G conditions, with repeatable sample sizes and no masked dependency failures.

A test case will be marked Fail if any acceptance condition is violated, if expected side-effect suppression is not demonstrable, if observability evidence is inconclusive, or if the environment produces ambiguous results that invalidate trust in the outcome. Soft failures such as intermittent stale-cache rendering, fluctuating p95 breaches, or inconsistent crop persistence are still treated as Fail because they directly undermine release readiness. Blocked status may be used only when execution could not proceed due to a confirmed external dependency issue, unavailable environment, or missing test data; blocked tests require explicit notes and rerun commitment.

All results will be recorded in Jira-linked execution runs with attachments or links to logs, screenshots, browser videos, API payload excerpts, performance summaries, and security scan artifacts. Manual test records must include tester identity, environment, timestamp, and observed versus expected notes. Automated records must include pipeline run IDs, commit hash, environment variables of interest, and trace IDs. This level of recording ensures that pass/fail classification is auditable and reproducible.

### 4.4 Defect Tracking
Defect tracking will be managed in Jira using a standardized workflow designed to support fast triage, clear accountability, and release-risk visibility. Defects discovered through manual execution, automated pipeline failures, OWASP ZAP scans, or performance threshold breaches will be created with mandatory fields including issue key linkage to AD-488, environment, build version, severity, reproducibility, affected requirement IDs, evidence attachments, and suspected layer of origin. Severity definitions will be explicit: Critical for security bypass, unauthorized downstream invocation, malware bypass, or production-blocking SLA failure; High for incorrect gateway rejection, stale avatar propagation, crop persistence corruption, or deterministic multi-view sync failures; Medium for localized UI errors or recoverable operational defects; Low for cosmetic or non-blocking issues.

The Jira workflow will progress through New, Triaged, In Progress, Ready for QA, Retest, Verified, Deferred, or Closed. Triage will occur daily for active work and immediately for Critical findings. Pull requests that claim a defect fix must reference the Jira defect ID, include impacted test evidence, and trigger the relevant automated suites. No defect may be moved to Verified without retest evidence from the layer that originally exposed it and, where applicable, regression confirmation from adjacent areas. For example, a fix for 5.01MB rejection must be retested not only for the API response but also for UI banner presentation and absence of downstream side effects.

Peer review is mandatory before closure of High and Critical defects. Release management will maintain a defect burndown and blocker dashboard during the readiness window. Any waived defect must document business rationale, compensating controls, expiry date, and approval authority. This process ensures defects are not just logged, but actively managed as release-risk objects.

### 4.5 Test Data Management

#### Data Requirements
<Identify the type, volume, and data source of test data required, along with masking, scrubbing, and seeding strategies.>

Test data for AD-488 must be intentionally engineered because the highest-risk scenarios depend on precise binary characteristics, token states, and propagation visibility. Required data includes valid JPEG and WebP image files of varied dimensions, exact-threshold 5.00MB files, controlled 5.01MB and larger files, malformed header samples, spoofed-extension files such as renamed text or script payloads, safe malware-signature test files approved for scanner validation, invalid crop parameter payloads, and profile accounts with preconfigured session states representing fresh token, expired token, and absent token conditions. Additional data sets must support multi-session synchronization checks, allowing the same user profile to be viewed simultaneously across different browser sessions and components.

Data sourcing will combine synthetic generation and controlled fixture repositories. No production user images or sensitive personal files will be used. File fixtures will be generated and hash-verified so their size, format, and header conditions remain stable across runs. Session tokens will be provisioned via test identity services and rotated according to environment policy. Where logs or profile metadata include personal identifiers, masking and scrub policies will be applied before evidence is exported outside the secure test environment. Seed scripts will create disposable test accounts and reset avatar state between runs to avoid stale-cache contamination.

Data governance is also required for performance validation. JMeter datasets must include a representative but controlled range of valid upload binaries so that repeated runs do not create skew from duplicate object handling or cache behavior. QA will maintain a catalog of approved fixtures and record any additions via peer-reviewed repository commits. This ensures repeatability and auditability across all critical scenarios.

