### 4.6 Test Environments
<Provide hardware, software, and configuration requirements for each target runtime layout.>

The test environment model includes Dev for early validation, QA for integrated behavior and negative/security execution, UAT for controlled business confirmation, Pre-Prod for production-like release qualification, and Prod for smoke verification. Each environment must provide access to the authenticated web application, the API Gateway route for avatar upload, server-side processing services with synchronous malware scanning enabled, the authorized cloud storage bucket path, observable CDN invalidation hooks, and a telemetry stack capable of correlating request IDs across layers. Without full observability, several critical acceptance criteria cannot be conclusively validated.

Browser execution nodes must support Playwright with video capture, HAR collection, and DOM mutation observation. API runners must support PyTest, multipart payload transmission, and secure token injection. Performance nodes require JMeter plus tcconfig capabilities to impose standard 4G throttling. Security validation requires OWASP ZAP integrated either as a sidecar scan service or dedicated runner with authenticated session support. Fault-injection capabilities should exist in QA and Pre-Prod to simulate scanner unavailability, storage delay, and CDN purge lag. A centralized audit log aggregator with tamper-evident storage is required for proving sequence correctness and absence of unauthorized downstream invocation.

Configuration parity is essential. Bucket naming, routing policy, scanner toggles, cache invalidation mechanisms, and authentication token behavior must resemble production as closely as possible in QA and Pre-Prod. Environment drift will be tracked as a release risk because it can invalidate both performance and security conclusions.

### 4.7 Test Automation Strategy
The automation strategy is risk-based and layered, using each framework where it provides the strongest evidence with the least ambiguity. Playwright will serve as the primary browser automation framework for UI state validation, crop interaction flows, session-aware visibility checks, drag-and-drop or file input handling, DOM mutation observation, and no-page-reload verification. Its architectural purpose is to verify end-user behavior at the browser boundary while also collecting artifacts such as screenshots, trace files, and videos. Playwright suites will be optimized into smoke, regression, and synchronization packs, with smoke scenarios executed on pull requests and broader suites scheduled nightly or on release branches.

PyTest will serve as the primary API and service-validation framework because it supports deterministic request composition, strong assertion patterns, modular fixtures, and straightforward integration with log-correlation helpers. It will validate gateway rejection, exact payload size boundaries, MIME and magic-number behavior, crop parameter APIs where exposed, storage destination controls, and side-effect suppression. PyTest is also the preferred mechanism for proving that requests rejected at the gateway do not trigger scanner or storage invocation, since API assertions can be paired with observability checks at fine granularity.

JMeter will be used to model performance and SLA behavior for the complete upload-to-distribution path. It will execute controlled volume patterns, gather percentile metrics, and export timing evidence for p50/p95/p99 analysis. tcconfig will work in tandem with JMeter, PyTest, and Playwright to enforce deterministic 4G network conditions so SLA validation is auditable rather than anecdotal. OWASP ZAP will be integrated into QA and Pre-Prod for authenticated scanning, negative probing of upload endpoints, and validation of session-boundary hardening. Collectively, these tools create a balanced automation architecture covering browser, API, security, and performance concerns without forcing a single tool to prove unsuitable requirements.

CI/CD integration rules are mandatory. Every pull request affecting relevant components must run a targeted automated quality pack before merge. Nightly jobs run expanded regression and negative packs. Release branch and Pre-Prod pipelines run SLA and security packs, and pipeline publication pauses at Human-in-the-Loop Gate 3 until manual validation is granted. Peer review of test automation code is enforced via branch protection, and flaky test quarantine requires documented approval to avoid silent coverage loss.

### 4.8 Test Reporting
Test reporting will combine pipeline-native reports, Jira execution summaries, and release dashboards that aggregate requirement coverage, defect posture, performance trends, and security findings. Reporting must distinguish between business objective status and technical control status so stakeholders can see whether a failure affects user visibility, security enforcement, data integrity, or timing SLA. Daily QA reporting during active execution will summarize total planned cases, executed counts, pass/fail/blocked status, open defects by severity, flaky automation incidents, environment issues, and top residual risks.

Release-level reporting will include traceability completion, pass/fail trends by test layer, p95 SLA measurements under 4G conditions, unauthorized request suppression evidence, malware-ordering verification, crop persistence results, and CDN synchronization status. Any waived issues or unresolved risk will be presented with business impact and compensating controls. This level of reporting supports a go/no-go decision that is evidence based rather than anecdotal.

#### 4.8.2 Maintaining Test Results
<Indicate where and how long historical test execution logs are stored and tracked.>

Historical results will be stored in the CI platform artifact repository, the centralized observability stack, and Jira-linked execution records. Browser traces, screenshots, and videos from Playwright; API logs and response captures from PyTest; JMeter reports; ZAP findings; and environment logs will be retained according to enterprise retention policy, with release-critical artifacts preserved for a minimum of two release cycles or the applicable audit window, whichever is longer. Storage locations must support immutable or append-only policies for critical evidence associated with security and release approvals.

Each retained artifact will include metadata such as pipeline run ID, branch, commit hash, environment, timestamp, and requirement trace IDs. This allows historical comparisons across builds and supports root-cause analysis for intermittent defects or regressions. Access to historical evidence will be role controlled, but visibility must remain sufficient for QA, engineering, product, and audit stakeholders participating in release investigation or post-incident review.

