Gate 3 review and publication | 1 business day | QA Director + Release Manager | Human approval recorded, residual risks accepted or mitigated, release recommendation issued

## 7. Entry and Exit Criteria
Entry criteria include approved requirements and scope boundaries, available QA and Pre-Prod environments, enabled observability across gateway/scanner/storage/CDN services, access to fixture repositories and seeded accounts, and baseline CI/CD jobs configured for Playwright, PyTest, JMeter, OWASP ZAP, and tcconfig usage. Exit criteria require full execution of critical and high-priority scenarios, no open Critical defects, documented disposition for all High defects, successful completion of required PR and release pipeline checks, validated traceability for all mapped requirements, and approved Gate 3 sign-off.

## 8. Assumptions and Dependencies
This strategy assumes the authentication service can issue controllable fresh and expired token states, the API Gateway exposes observable request identifiers, the malware scanner can process approved test signatures safely, the storage tier and CDN expose event hooks or logs sufficient for correlation, and the crop implementation is accessible via the standard upload workflow or supporting service contract. Dependencies include environment parity, stable access to authorized cloud buckets, reliable cache invalidation telemetry, and timely development support for defects and instrumentation gaps.

## 9. Risk Mitigation Table
Risk ID | Risk Description | Probability | Impact | Severity | Mitigation / Contingency | Owner
---|---|---|---|---|---|---
R1 | Authentication token expiration edge cases may cause inconsistent UI gating and allow requests to reach the gateway during stale-cookie windows, producing nondeterministic 401 behavior and possible downstream load. | Medium | High | High | 1) Create deterministic fresh/expired/absent token fixtures. 2) Add Playwright checks for hidden/disabled UI plus PyTest checks for gateway 401 responses. 3) Correlate gateway logs to confirm rejected requests do not invoke scanner, storage, or CDN services. 4) Add PR gate tests around session middleware changes. 5) Monitor post-release auth rejection metrics for anomaly spikes. | QA Lead / Backend Lead
R2 | Precise 5.00MB versus 5.01MB multipart payload handling may be misclassified due to encoding overhead, causing false acceptance or rejection and breaching boundary requirements. | Medium | High | High | 1) Generate hash-verified binary fixtures with measured payload sizes and transport-aware request construction. 2) Use PyTest to validate server-observed size and gateway decision point. 3) Add UI checks for required interface error banners on oversize files. 4) Include regression tests for exact-threshold acceptance and immediate rejection. 5) Document tolerance assumptions explicitly if gateway measures full multipart size. | SDET / API Lead
R3 | Mock or integrated malware scanning service desynchronization may allow storage attempts before scan completion or produce ambiguous outcomes under latency spikes. | Low | Critical | High | 1) Use observability hooks to validate scan-before-store ordering. 2) Inject scanner delay/failure in QA to verify safe blocking behavior. 3) Fail release if storage writes occur without preceding clean-scan evidence. 4) Retain audit logs with trace IDs for all upload attempts. 5) Establish operational alerting for scan timeout or bypass anomalies. | Security QA / Platform Lead
R4 | CDN purge lag or cache invalidation race conditions may cause stale avatars in profile grids or header bars even when storage succeeds, violating immediate synchronization expectations. | Medium | High | High | 1) Add multi-session Playwright synchronization checks across profile and header surfaces. 2) Correlate storage success timestamps with purge events and rendered update times. 3) Test cold/warm cache scenarios in Pre-Prod. 4) Define rollback or user-message contingency if purge delay exceeds threshold. 5) Add monitoring on purge latency and stale-hit ratios post-release. | Frontend Lead / Release Manager
R5 | Server-side crop processing may diverge from preview behavior or accept invalid crop coordinates, resulting in incorrect persisted avatars or broken rendering. | Medium | Medium | Medium | 1) Build explicit crop functional, boundary, and negative tests. 2) Compare preview dimensions and final persisted output using deterministic fixtures. 3) Validate invalid coordinates, aspect anomalies, and out-of-bounds values are safely rejected. 4) Include regression coverage for crop persistence across refresh and session changes. 5) Require peer review for crop-service changes and image-handling libraries. | QA Lead / Image Service Owner
R6 | End-to-end SLA of 2.0 seconds under standard 4G may fail intermittently because of cumulative latency across gateway, scanner, storage, and CDN propagation, creating release instability. | Medium | Critical | Critical | 1) Use tcconfig and JMeter to run repeatable timed scenarios under controlled 4G constraints. 2) Measure p50/p95/p99 and stage-level timings to isolate bottlenecks. 3) Set release gate thresholds in Pre-Prod and block promotion on deterministic breach. 4) Execute warm and cold path runs to expose cache-related variability. 5) Coordinate remediation with platform teams before go-live if budget erosion exceeds tolerance. | Performance Engineer / Release Manager
R7 | Unauthorized storage destination or configuration drift between QA/Pre-Prod and production may mask routing defects and invalidate release confidence. | Low | High | Medium | 1) Validate bucket path and authorized cloud destination using PyTest assertions and infrastructure metadata checks. 2) Add environment configuration review to readiness checklist. 3) Compare routing, scanner, and CDN settings across QA and Pre-Prod before final sign-off. 4) Block release on unresolved drift affecting security or timing conclusions. 5) Archive configuration snapshots with release evidence. | Release Engineering / DevOps

